Title: Erg struct: schema-literal fields, drop accessors and the headers map
Created: 2026-05-11
Author: claude-sonnet-4-6

--- log ---
2026-05-11T00:00Z claude-sonnet-4-6 created

2026-05-11T00:00Z MinhHaDuong note partially reverses 0010 (which added the strict separator-duplicate rule) — this ticket relaxes that to first-occurrence semantics

--- body ---
## Context
This is the unfinished half of ticket 0105. PR #115 added accessor methods
and unexported the headers field, but the struct itself still stores values
in a `map[string][]string` bag and the accessors do runtime string-keyed
lookups. The struct does not mirror the spec's header table; rule checks
on a typo like `"Blocked-By"` would silently miss values.

The spec defines a closed set of 6 headers; the struct should reflect it
directly with typed fields, and the trivial accessor methods that exist
only as map wrappers go away.

## Current shape (verbatim, erg.go:121-135)
```go
type Erg struct {
    Path     string
    headers  map[string][]string
    LogLines []string
    Body     string
    HasMagic bool
    LogSepCount  int
    BodySepCount int
    ClosedInLog  bool
    ClosedInBody bool
}
```

## Proposed shape
The diagnostic trace becomes a sibling struct returned alongside `Erg`,
so `Erg` is schema-pure from day 1 (not "TEMPORARY"). In follow-up 0117,
`ParseDiagnostics` is replaced in-place by `[]ParseError` — the parser
signature stays "two return values" across the staged refactors.

```go
// Line is a single-line string: no embedded newlines.
// Enforced by the parser invariant (header values read up to LF).
type Line = string

// Erg is the schema-literal projection of a %erg v1 ticket.
type Erg struct {
    Path     Line
    HasMagic bool

    // v1 headers — typed fields populated from first occurrence
    Title      Line   // required, non-empty (spec tightened — see below)
    Created    Line   // required, non-empty
    Author     Line   // required, non-empty
    Closed     Line   // optional; non-empty when present
    BlockedBys []Line // possibly empty; one entry per `Blocked-by:` line
    Tags       []Line // possibly empty; one entry per `Tag:` line (after 0106)

    LogLines []Line   // one structured event per entry
    Body     string   // multiline
}

// ParseDiagnostics carries parser observations the validator consumes.
// In 0117 (parse+validate merge) this struct is replaced in-place by
// []ParseError; until then, validate.go walks these fields.
type ParseDiagnostics struct {
    Unknown            []Line  // unknown header keys seen
    RepeatedSingletons []Line  // singleton keys seen more than once
    ClosedEmpty        bool    // ANY `Closed:` line seen with empty value
    ClosedInLog        bool
    ClosedInBody       bool
    HasLogSep          bool    // `--- log ---` was seen at least once
    HasBodySep         bool    // `--- body ---` was seen at least once
}

func parseErg(path string) (Erg, ParseDiagnostics)
```

`Line` is a type alias (not a distinct type) — interchangeable with
`string` at call sites, but it documents intent at every declaration
and shows up in godoc/IDE hover. The parser already enforces the
no-newline invariant by construction (line-based read loop); the alias
is documentation, not enforcement.

Note plural field names `BlockedBys` and `Tags`: the field is a list of
lines (possibly empty). Header name in the file remains singular
(`Blocked-by:`, `Tag:` after 0106) per the repeatable-header convention.

## Parser invariants (pinned)
The implementer must preserve these semantics; they are easy to break
when moving from the map bag to typed fields.

- **Tags trimming**: today's `Tags()` accessor trims values and drops
  empty entries (erg.go:202-209). Parser must apply the same when
  appending to `Tags` — trim, skip empty after trim.
- **`HasLogSep` / `HasBodySep`**: set to true on **any** sighting of
  the separator literal, regardless of whether the parser transitions
  sections on that sighting. Set unconditionally; do not gate behind
  the first-occurrence transition.
- **`ClosedEmpty`**: set to true if **any** `Closed:` line appears with
  an empty (post-trim) value, not only the first occurrence. A
  `Closed: ok` followed by `Closed: ` must still produce the empty-value
  error from validator rule 4. (The repeated-singleton rule also fires
  via `RepeatedSingletons`, but the empty-value signal is independent.)
- **`Closed` field stores the first non-empty value** (matching today's
  `Closed()` behavior). If only empty `Closed:` lines appear,
  `Closed = ""` and `ClosedEmpty = true`.

## Spec tightening (required headers)
Update `tickets/spec-erg-v1.md`: required headers (`Title`, `Created`,
`Author`) must have a non-empty value. Today the validator passes
`Title: ` (key present, value empty) because rule 2 only checks key
presence. After this refactor, empty required headers fail validation.

## Spec clarification (line-string cardinality)
Update `tickets/spec-erg-v1.md`:
- §Headers (table): add prose immediately after the table noting that
  **all header values are line-strings — single line, no embedded
  newlines**. Multiline content belongs in the body section.
- §Headers Type column: distinguish `line` (single line) from freeform
  string. Today every header is typed `string` regardless of cardinality.
- §Log already says "Each line records one event" — no change needed
  there.

## Spec relaxation (separator duplicates)
Validator rule 11 (numbered rule 12 in some prior drafts; canonical
numbering is in validate.go) today requires each separator
(`--- log ---`, `--- body ---`) to appear **exactly once**. This is
overstrict: legitimate bodies will contain those literals — heredoc
examples, format documentation, this very ticket's body that quotes the
strings. The parser already uses first-occurrence semantics for section
transitions (erg.go:357-369), but the validator rejects the file based
on the count.

Change:
- Drop the "appears N times" branch of rule 11; keep only "missing".
- §Structure rewording: "Both separators are required" becomes "The
  first `--- log ---` and the first `--- body ---` (in order) are the
  section separators. Subsequent occurrences are body text."
- Validator rules text in `const helpValidate` (validate.go, since
  PR #121) updated to match.
- Struct change: replace `LogSepCount`/`BodySepCount` (which existed
  only to drive the now-deleted overstrict check) with
  `HasLogSep`/`HasBodySep` bools in `ParseDiagnostics`.
- This partially reverses ticket 0010 (logged above) — that ticket
  added the strict rule; this one relaxes it. The new rule still
  rejects truly malformed input (separator missing).

Known wart left untouched: `isClosedHeaderLine` (erg.go:416) still
fires on any body line starting `Closed:` at column 0, even when the
line is body prose discussing the format. This pre-existed and remains
out of scope; a body that quotes the separator literals is fine, but
quoting `Closed: <reason>` flush-left will still be flagged. Worth a
follow-up ticket if it bites.

## Accessor methods go away
All trivial accessors are deleted; call sites read the field directly.
Two methods with real logic survive — but the `Closed()` method is
**renamed `IsClosed()`** to avoid shadowing the new `Closed` field
(Go allows it but readers trip; `t.Closed` returns a string, `t.Closed()`
would return a bool — same identifier, different types).

- `IsClosed()` — path-based test OR non-empty `Closed` field
- `BlockedByRefs()` — parses every raw ref string

| Today               | After                                          |
|---------------------|------------------------------------------------|
| `t.Title()`         | `t.Title` (direct read)                        |
| `t.Created()`       | `t.Created`                                    |
| `t.Author()`        | `t.Author`                                     |
| `t.ClosedHeader()`  | `t.Closed != ""`                               |
| `t.BlockedBy()`     | `t.BlockedBys`                                 |
| `t.Tags()`          | `t.Tags`                                       |
| `t.Closed()`        | `t.IsClosed()` (renamed; same logic)           |
| `t.BlockedByRefs()` | unchanged method, iterates `t.BlockedBys`      |

## Call-site impact
21 accessor call sites across 6 files. After this refactor:
- archive.go: `Closed()` → `IsClosed()`; `BlockedByRefs()` stays; `ClosedHeader()` → `t.Closed != ""`
- check.go:   `ClosedHeader()` → `t.Closed != ""`; `Closed()` ×2 → `IsClosed()`; `BlockedByRefs()` stays
- close.go:   `Closed()` ×2 → `IsClosed()`; `BlockedBy()` → `t.BlockedBys`
- ready.go:   `Closed()` ×2 → `IsClosed()`; `Tags()` → `t.Tags`; `BlockedByRefs()` stays; `Title()` → `t.Title`
- validate.go: `Created()` → `t.Created`; `BlockedByRefs()` ×2 stays; the 4 direct `t.headers[...]` accesses switch to typed fields on `Erg` and bools on `ParseDiagnostics`

## Dead code retirement
After the refactor, these constants/maps in `validate.go` become
unreachable and must be removed in the same PR:
- `RequiredHeaders` (was driving rule 2 via map lookup)
- `SingletonHeaders` (was driving rule 3 singleton check)
- `ValidHeaders` (was driving rule 3 unknown-header check)
- `ValidTagValues` stays — it backs the Tags closed-set enumeration
  rule, which still applies.

## Test impact
- erg_test.go: parser tests construct `Erg` literals — update to new
  shape. Parser tests now also assert `ParseDiagnostics` content where
  relevant.
- validate_test.go: rule 2 fixtures adjusted for empty-required-header
  tightening; add a positive test for it. Add a `Closed:` duplicate
  empty-value test (`Closed: x\nClosed: ` must trigger both repeated-
  singleton AND empty-value errors).
- New fixture: a body containing `--- log ---` and `--- body ---` as
  text. Assertions:
  (a) validator accepts the file;
  (b) `erg.Body` preserves the literal lines verbatim (no silent loss);
  (c) `ParseDiagnostics.HasLogSep` and `.HasBodySep` are true.

## Out of scope (held back from 0105's scope creep)
- **Parser/validator merge into one pass.** The diagnostic-trace fields
  (`Unknown`, `RepeatedSingletons`, `ClosedEmpty`, `ClosedInLog`,
  `ClosedInBody`, `HasLogSep`, `HasBodySep`) are placeholders that
  disappear in that follow-up. File as a separate ticket when ready.
- **Type tightening of header fields**: `BlockedBys []string` → `[]Ref`,
  `Created string` → `time.Time`. These eliminate the surviving
  `BlockedByRefs()` method and push date parsing into the parser. File
  as a separate ticket after the merge ticket lands (the merge is the
  natural place to introduce parse-time type errors).
- Renaming `Tags:` → `Tag:` (header rename) is ticket 0106 / PR #122.
  This ticket keeps the Go field name `Tags []Line` (idiomatic plural
  slice) regardless of which order they merge.
- Function-level splits in `parseErg`; only struct shape and the
  accessor surface change.

## Endpoint shape (post-merge, post-type-tightening)
This ticket plus the two follow-ups land at:

```go
type Line = string

type Erg struct {
    Path     Line
    HasMagic bool

    Title      Line       // required, non-empty
    Created    time.Time  // required, parsed at parse time
    Author     Line       // required, non-empty
    Closed     Line       // optional; non-empty when present
    BlockedBys []Ref      // possibly empty; each ref parsed at parse time
    Tags       []Line     // possibly empty

    LogLines []Line       // one structured event per entry
    Body     string       // multiline
}

func parseErg(path string) (Erg, []ParseError)
```

Only `IsClosed()` survives as a method (path-OR-header is intrinsic;
keep as method or convert to free function — taste). `BlockedByRefs()`
and `ParseDiagnostics` both vanish: refs are pre-parsed at parse time
(0118), diagnostics become parse errors (0117). The struct is a literal
projection of the spec.

## Dependencies
- Ticket 0115 (split Ref into ref.go) has **landed** (PR #120). This
  ticket now touches only the Erg portion of erg.go; `BlockedBys` stays
  `[]Line` here, with Ref pre-parsing deferred to 0118.
- Ticket 0114 (help strings into command files) has **landed**
  (PR #121). The validator help text (rule-11/12 wording) now lives in
  `const helpValidate` in `validate.go` — NOT in `helptext.go` (which
  is now a thin registry). Spec/help edits in this ticket target
  `helpValidate`.
- Ticket 0106 (rename `Tags:` → `Tag:`) is **open in PR #122**.
  Resolution depends on merge order:
  - If 0106 merges first: this ticket reads `Tag:` lines into the
    `Tags` field (idiomatic Go plural slice). The "Today" column of
    the accessor-elimination table refers to `t.Tag()` (not `Tags()`).
  - If this ticket merges first: 0106 is rebased to delete the
    `Tag:`-vs-`Tags:` map-key constants on top of the schema-literal
    fields; rename is then a one-liner on the field comment and the
    parser key string.
  - PR #122 was branched before PR #120 and currently re-introduces
    Ref code into erg.go. It will need a rebase against main
    regardless of 0116's timing.
- This ticket bundles three changes deliberately (struct refactor +
  rule 2 tightening + rule 11 relaxation) so the spec and code stay
  synced in a single review.

## Exit criteria
- `Erg` struct contains exactly the schema-pure fields: `Path`,
  `HasMagic`, `Title`, `Created`, `Author`, `Closed`, `BlockedBys`,
  `Tags`, `LogLines`, `Body`. Nothing else.
- `ParseDiagnostics` carries the trace fields; `parseErg` returns
  `(Erg, ParseDiagnostics)`.
- All trivial accessor methods are removed; only `IsClosed()` (renamed
  from `Closed()`) and `BlockedByRefs()` remain.
- `type Line = string` alias declared; line-string fields use `Line`,
  multiline `Body` stays `string`.
- Dead constants removed: `RequiredHeaders`, `SingletonHeaders`,
  `ValidHeaders` in validate.go.
- Spec updates land in the same PR:
  - §Headers: line-string cardinality stated; Type column distinguishes
    line vs. freeform.
  - §Structure: separator-duplicates relaxed to first-occurrence.
  - §Headers + helpText: required headers (Title, Created, Author) must
    be non-empty.
- `make test` passes; new tests assert:
  (a) empty required header is rejected;
  (b) body containing `--- log ---`/`--- body ---` literals is accepted
      AND `erg.Body` preserves them verbatim;
  (c) `Closed: x\nClosed: ` triggers both repeated-singleton and
      empty-value errors.
